WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.837
Use-after-free prevention in Rust is a
layered engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:05.697
Prefer safe ownership and borrowing.

3
00:00:05.777 --> 00:00:09.004
Know who owns each value, who may borrow
it, and when cleanup happens.

4
00:00:09.084 --> 00:00:12.644
Keep lifetime relationships visible in
function signatures, type design,

5
00:00:12.724 --> 00:00:13.836
and module boundaries.

6
00:00:13.916 --> 00:00:16.530
Return owned data when no valid borrowed
owner remains.

7
00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.835
Avoid references into short-lived
internals.

8
00:00:18.915 --> 00:00:22.642
Encapsulate internal state so callers
cannot depend on temporary locations or

9
00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:23.557
invalid handles.

10
00:00:23.637 --> 00:00:27.197
Use constructors and domain types to
represent valid owned state instead of

11
00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:29.613
spreading lifetime assumptions across
callers.

12
00:00:29.693 --> 00:00:32.196
Use shared ownership only when it matches
the design.

13
00:00:32.276 --> 00:00:35.224
Understand how `Rc`, `Arc`, and `Weak`
affect cleanup timing.

14
00:00:35.304 --> 00:00:37.307
Make async and thread ownership explicit.

15
00:00:37.387 --> 00:00:40.502
Define cancellation and shutdown behavior
for long-lived tasks.

16
00:00:40.582 --> 00:00:43.029
Keep shared mutation synchronized and
reviewable.

17
00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:47.058
Keep unsafe and FFI boundaries small,
documented, tested, and wrapped in safe APIs.

18
00:00:47.138 --> 00:00:49.808
Review callbacks, raw pointers, allocation
contracts,

19
00:00:49.888 --> 00:00:53.226
deallocation responsibility, retained
pointers, and cleanup paths.

20
00:00:53.306 --> 00:00:56.532
The wrapper should prevent safe callers
from creating stale access.

21
00:00:56.612 --> 00:00:58.837
Finally, maintain lifecycle safety over
time.

22
00:00:58.917 --> 00:01:02.755
Test construction, transfer, shutdown,
cancellation, failed initialization,

23
00:01:02.835 --> 00:01:03.892
and regression cases.

24
00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:05.863
Update safety contracts as APIs evolve.

25
00:01:05.943 --> 00:01:09.058
The goal is Rust code where stale access
is prevented by ownership,

26
00:01:09.138 --> 00:01:12.420
made visible by lifetimes, and contained
by careful boundary design.
